\section{Regions and the noise floor across heads}\label{sec:S6}

Unless stated otherwise, $D$ is the dense optically pumped magnetometer (OPM) array's detectability relative to Neuromag's << F.g2_squid_channels >> channels in dB (Section~\ref{sec:S1}), with sensor plus brain noise. It is the area-weighted median over the cortical targets of a head or region (both hemispheres). The helmets are those of Section~\ref{sec:S5}. The << F.wr_n_smaller_heads_words >> smaller heads are the << F.wr_n_scaled_words >> scaled adults, the << F.wr_n_templates_words >> infant templates and the << F.wr_n_children_words >> school-aged children. The main text draws its pediatric conclusions from the scaled adults and the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month templates, the principal smaller heads. It reports the other << F.wr_n_provisional_words >>, the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template and the children, separately (Section~\ref{sec:S10}). Ranges and counts here over all smaller heads, or over all templates, include those << F.wr_n_provisional_words >>.

\subsection{Regions by head}\label{sec:S6.1}

In the fixed adult helmet at top contact, the dense array's detectability was higher in every region of every head ($D$ << F.regh_top_vs_combined_cells_range >> dB over the cells of Fig.~\ref{fig:S9}A). A smaller head exceeded the adult's value for the same region in << F.regh_top_vs_combined_smaller_cells_above_adult_count >> of << F.regh_top_vs_combined_smaller_cells_n >> cells. Of the six lobes, the temporal lobe gained most in the principal templates (<< F.regh_infant2yr_top_vs_combined_temporal >> and << F.regh_infant12mo_top_vs_combined_temporal >> dB at << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >> months, against << F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_temporal >> dB in the adult). It did so too in the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template (<< F.regh_infant18mo_top_vs_combined_temporal >> dB).

The parahippocampal and mesial temporal rows, the adult's lowest temporal rows (<< F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_parahippocampal >> and << F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal >> dB), were higher in every principal smaller head and in the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template. The mesial temporal group reached << F.regh_principal_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal_range >> dB in the principal smaller heads and << F.regh_infant18mo_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal >> dB in the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template. In the children it reached << F.regh_children_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal_range >> dB, child C (<< F.regh_child_c_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal >> dB) lying below the adult. The cingulate was the lowest row in every head: << F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_cingulate >> dB in the adult, << F.regh_principal_top_vs_combined_cingulate_range >> dB in the principal smaller heads and << F.regh_provisional_top_vs_combined_cingulate_range >> dB in the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10}.

The peak-channel signal-to-noise ratio (SNR; sensor plus brain noise) gave a different picture. Against the magnetometers alone, the dense array's peak-channel SNR was higher in every head. It was << F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_mag_ib_peak_d >> dB in the adult at top contact, << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_mag_ib_peak_d_range >> dB in the principal smaller heads and << F.g3b_provisional_dense_vs_mag_ib_peak_d_range >> dB in the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10}. Against Neuromag's best channel, the dense array's peak-channel SNR was lower in the adult (<< F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >> dB) and in each child (<< F.g3b_child_a_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >>, << F.g3b_child_b_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >> and << F.g3b_child_c_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >> dB in A, B and C). In the principal smaller heads it ranged from << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d_range >> dB, and in the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template it was << F.g3b_infant18mo_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >> dB.

In the helmet scaled with the head (lobes only; Fig.~\ref{fig:S9}B), the regional gain was smaller, by how much depending on the reference. Of the << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_smaller_lobe_cells_n >> lobe cells of the smaller heads, << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_smaller_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_count >> were at or below the adult's in its own scaled helmet and << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_smaller_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_top_count >> at or below the adult's at top contact. Against the adult at top contact, the counts were << F.wr_cfx_principal_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_top_count >> of << F.wr_cfx_principal_lobe_cells_n >> for the principal smaller heads, << F.wr_cfx_infant18mo_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_top_count >> of << F.wr_cfx_infant18mo_lobe_cells_n >> for the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template and << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_children_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_top_count >> of << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_children_lobe_cells_n >> for the children. The counts compare point estimates, without a tolerance. The children's lobes ranged from << F.regh_children_cfx_vs_combined_lobes_range >> dB in the scaled helmet.

In the helmet fitted at the adult's gap (Table~\ref{tab:S8}), the scaled adults kept the adult's ordering: frontal, precentral and superior temporal cortex highest, cingulate and insula lowest. The cingulate was the lowest row in every head except the children, where the insula lay just below it. The templates and children reordered the upper rows. The parahippocampal and mesial temporal rows were high in the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template and, of the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10}, in the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template and child B; the occipital lobe was highest in child C.

The children's regional point estimates lay on both sides of the adult's. Child B was below the adult in the frontal lobe (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_frontal >> dB) and the precentral gyrus (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_precentral >> dB). It was above the adult in the parahippocampal (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_parahippocampal >> dB) and occipital (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_occipital >> dB) cortex. Child C was below the adult in the entorhinal cortex (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_entorhinal >> dB) but above it in the occipital lobe (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_occipital >> dB).

The cortical maps (Fig.~\ref{fig:S10}; main text, Fig. 8C) show $D$ largest on the lateral convexity, most on gyral crowns, with medial surfaces near zero. By region, then, the ordering changed with the helmet; the relative weight of helmet and region was not quantified. Heat maps and cortical maps show point estimates, and the pediatric intervals represent within-anatomy resampling only.

