\subsection{The noise floor across heads}\label{sec:S6.2}

In every head, $D$ fell as the OPM's sensor noise rose (Fig.~\ref{fig:S11}); these sensitivity values are medians without intervals (Table~\ref{tab:S16}). At << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >> fT/√Hz, the upper end of the sweep (Section~\ref{sec:S8.3}), the dense array's $D$ was << F.g3b_adult_asd30_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB in the adult but stayed positive in every smaller head. It was << F.g3b_principal_asd30_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB in the principal smaller heads and << F.g3b_provisional_asd30_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB in the << F.wr_n_provisional_words >> heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10}. The site-matched array's $D$ was negative in every head except the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_years >>-year scaled adult. It was << F.g3b_adult_asd30_matched_vs_combined_ib_d >> dB in the adult, << F.g3b_principal_asd30_matched_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB in the principal smaller heads and << F.g3b_provisional_asd30_matched_vs_combined_ib_d_range >> dB in the heads of Section~\ref{sec:S10}.

The difference from the adult also depended on the noise. The principal smaller heads were above the adult at both << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_min >> and << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >> fT/√Hz (<< F.g3b_principal_asd7_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> and << F.g3b_principal_asd30_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> dB, differences of medians). The individual children were slightly below the adult at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_min >> fT/√Hz (<< F.g3b_children_asd7_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> dB) and above it at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >> fT/√Hz (<< F.g3b_children_asd30_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> dB). A plausible reading, not tested here, is that the children's OPM channels, dominated by brain noise, lose less to a noisier sensor than the adult's.

